# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repository, on every platform.
#
# This file exists rather than relying on a global config setting, because the
# rule has to travel with the repository. A machine with different settings
# still produces the right commits.
#
# Verify with the command below. The INDEX column is the half that decides what
# a commit contains, and every tracked text file should read i/lf:
#
#   git ls-files --eol
#
# The working-tree column is not uniform and does not need to be. A file written
# by an editor that did not read this file will disagree until it is next
# checked out. A check that compares the working tree against what this file
# resolves is the repair, and it produces no git diff because the index is LF
# on both sides.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.md            text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.jsonc         text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.xml           text eol=lf
*.html          text eol=lf
*.css           text eol=lf
*.sql           text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
*.zsh           text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.editorconfig   text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the one exception, and it is not a preference. Windows
# PowerShell 5.1 mis-parses a here-string whose terminator arrives with a bare
# LF, so these keep CRLF in the working tree.
#
# A second 5.1 requirement lives in the FILE rather than here: a .ps1 with any
# non-ASCII byte needs a UTF-8 BOM, because 5.1 decodes a BOM-less file as the
# system ANSI code page. `text` does not touch a BOM, so the two rules coexist.
# docs/conventions/shell.md section 8.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.psm1          text eol=crlf
*.psd1          text eol=crlf

# Batch files are read by cmd.exe, which wants CRLF.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf

# Anything whose exact bytes are the content. Changing one byte changes what
# the file IS, so no translation may be applied.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.webp          binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.zip           binary
*.gz            binary
*.bz2           binary
*.xz            binary
*.zst           binary
*.7z            binary
*.tar           binary
*.exe           binary
*.dll           binary
*.so            binary
*.dylib         binary
*.wasm          binary
*.bin           binary
*.iso           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ttf           binary
*.otf           binary
*.mp4           binary
*.webm          binary
*.mp3           binary

# Generated and noisy in a diff. Collapsed in review, still tracked.
*.lock          linguist-generated=true
package-lock.json   linguist-generated=true
pnpm-lock.yaml      linguist-generated=true
yarn.lock           linguist-generated=true
